and reliably sunny with no real storm season. December-April is peak (winter escape, highest prices); May-August is warm, quieter value and windiest (best for windsurf and Hi-Winds).","source":"onehappyisland.com","si_tag":"tropical"},{"q":"Which beach - Eagle or Palm?","a":"Both: Eagle Beach for the wide, quiet, top-rated sand and fofoti trees; Palm Beach for the resort strip, watersports and nightlife. Most people beach at Eagle and stay at Palm.","source":"onehappyisland.com","si_tag":"tropical"},{"q":"Do I need to be a diver to see the Antilla wreck?","a":"No - the water is so clear that snorkelers can see the 400-ft wreck from the surface in ~60ft, and most excursions include boat, snorkel gear and guiding. Certified divers penetrate deeper sections (advanced); everyone else snorkels the shallows (no no-fly restriction).","source":"visitaruba.com","si_tag":"diveglobal"},{"q":"Is Aruba good for windsurfing or a wildlife day?","a":"Both are signatures. Hadicurari (Fisherman''s Huts) is a world-class windsurf/kite beach (Hi-Winds each June/July), and you can wade with flamingos on Renaissance Island or hike Arikok National Park to the Natural Pool - beyond the beach, Aruba has real range.","source":"aruba.com","si_tag":"ocean"}],"seo":{"meta_title":"Aruba Travel Guide: Eagle & Palm Beach, the Antilla Wreck, Arikok & Windsurfing","meta_description":"Plan Aruba - the Caribbean''s sure-thing sun, safely south of the hurricane belt: top-rated Eagle Beach, the 400-ft Antilla wreck (snorkelable), world-class Hadicurari windsurfing, Arikok National Park and the Natural Pool, and the flamingos of Renaissance Island.","slug":"aruba","primary_keyword":"Aruba travel guide","long_tail":["Eagle Beach Aruba","Antilla wreck dive snorkel","Aruba Hi-Winds windsurfing Hadicurari","Arikok National Park Natural Pool","Renaissance Island flamingos","is Aruba safe"],"search_language":"nl","local_keywords":["Aruba","One Happy Island","Eagle Beach","Palm Beach","Antilla","Hadicurari","Ar